From 6f4a3631351b27078288065ccd118035ad31d661 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 17:53:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: replace double-precision pixel blending with fixed-point

Peer review (session "dossage-24") flagged double-precision FP as a
likely render-loop cost after patches 0015/0016 fixed the loop's other
two known costs (worldX transcendental math, HashTable modulo) -- the
486's FPU-to-integer conversion requires a full rounding-mode save/
restore (fldcw/fistp/fldcw), not just the arithmetic itself, so a
(unsigned char)(double) cast is disproportionately expensive there.

Found the actual hot site by reading World.cpp directly rather than
converting everything indiscriminately: sampleFromWorld() (game.cpp
calls it twice per pixel, up to 2400 times/frame) does its weight
multiply and truncate to unsigned char UNCONDITIONALLY on every call --
including on a worldSampleHashTable cache HIT, which is the vast
majority of calls (this port's own real-hardware hit ratios run
94-99%). So the same cached rgbColor value was being re-truncated from
double to unsigned char on every single reuse, redundantly, rather than
once when it was first computed.

Fix: rgbColorStruct's r/g/b fields are now `int` (already-rounded 0-255
values), not `double`. The double blend math that PRODUCES a color
(tile-set blending, animation-page blending in
sampleFromWorldNoWeight()) is UNCHANGED -- it only runs on an actual
cache miss (~1-6% of calls per the same real-hardware hit ratios), so
it was never the target here -- it just now rounds to int once, at the
point of computation, instead of leaving a double for every future
reuse to re-truncate. sampleFromWorld()'s own weight parameter is now
an int scaled by 256 (256 == a weight of 1.0) instead of a double,
computed once per screen column in game.cpp's existing patch-0015
precompute loop (100 times/frame, not 2400) -- bWeight256 truncates the
same way the original double path did, aWeight256 = 256 - bWeight256
(not an independent truncation of 1-bWeight) so the pair still always
sums to exactly 256, matching the original aWeight = 1-bWeight identity
exactly rather than letting two separate roundings drift apart.
sampleFromWorld()'s per-call math is now `(inWeight256 * c.r) >> 8` --
pure 32-bit integer multiply-and-shift (max product 256*255=65280, no
overflow risk), not an FPU operation at all.

rgbColor is entirely internal to World.cpp (confirmed via a repo-wide
grep) -- no other file's contract changes, other than
sampleFromWorld()'s own declared signature in World.h (double -> int
weight parameter, updated at both real call sites in game.cpp plus the
dead/commented-out mural-generation code for consistency).

Verified under DOSBox-X with careful visual comparison (this touches
color computation across the whole rendering pipeline -- world tiles,
sprites, chests, gems -- not just an intermediate buffer): two
screenshots several seconds apart during normal scrolling gameplay show
identical terrain shading, path color, sprite colors, and status-bar
icon colors to every prior smoke test in this series -- no banding, no
channel shift, no truncation artifacts. Exit-time hit ratios
(94.6%/99.2%) match the established pattern, confirming the cache
itself still behaves correctly under the new int-based storage. Clean
boot/play/ESCAPE-quit, no hang. Real-hardware confirmation on the
486DX2-50 (fps number plus a repeat RENDERDB.LOG capture) is the next
step.
---
 gameSource/World.cpp | 96 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----------------
 gameSource/World.h   |  5 ++-
 gameSource/game.cpp  | 46 ++++++++++++++-------
 3 files changed, 96 insertions(+), 51 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/World.cpp b/gameSource/World.cpp
index 2906540..a77dbea 100644
--- a/gameSource/World.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/World.cpp
@@ -287,10 +287,26 @@ char isSpriteTransparent( GraphicContainer *inContainer, int inSpriteIndex ) {
 
 
 
+// DOS-PORT: int channels (0-255), not double. sampleFromWorld() below
+// runs unconditionally on every sampleFromWorld() call -- 2x per pixel,
+// up to 2400/frame on this port's 100x12 image, REGARDLESS of whether
+// the underlying value came from a fresh cache-miss compute or a cache
+// hit (the vast majority of calls, since the world scrolls and most
+// samples are already cached) -- and every one of those calls used to
+// pay a (unsigned char)(double) truncation per channel: real,
+// disproportionate cost on a 486, whose FPU-to-integer conversion
+// requires a full rounding-mode save/restore (fldcw/fistp/fldcw), not
+// just the multiply itself. Storing already-rounded ints here means
+// that conversion happens once, at cache-population time (a genuine
+// cache MISS only, ~5-8% of calls per this port's own measured hit
+// ratios), instead of redundantly on every reuse of the same cached
+// value. The double blend math that PRODUCES these values (tile-set
+// blending, animation-page blending) is untouched -- it's rare enough
+// (same cache-miss-only frequency) that it was never the target here.
 struct rgbColorStruct {
-    double r;
-    double g;
-    double b;
+    int r;
+    int g;
+    int b;
     };
 typedef struct rgbColorStruct rgbColor;
 
@@ -305,13 +321,18 @@ rgbColor sampleFromWorldNoWeightHash( int inX, int inY );
 
 
 
-Uint32 sampleFromWorld( int inX, int inY, double inWeight ) {
+Uint32 sampleFromWorld( int inX, int inY, int inWeight256 ) {
     rgbColor c = sampleFromWorldNoWeightHash( inX, inY );
-    
-    unsigned char r = (unsigned char)( inWeight * c.r );
-    unsigned char g = (unsigned char)( inWeight * c.g );
-    unsigned char b = (unsigned char)( inWeight * c.b );
-    
+
+    // DOS-PORT: inWeight256 * c.r maxes at 256*255=65280, well inside
+    // 32-bit int range -- no overflow risk. This is pure integer
+    // multiply-and-shift, not an FPU operation at all, unlike the
+    // (unsigned char)(double) truncation this replaced (see
+    // rgbColorStruct's own comment above for why that mattered).
+    unsigned char r = (unsigned char)( ( inWeight256 * c.r ) >> 8 );
+    unsigned char g = (unsigned char)( ( inWeight256 * c.g ) >> 8 );
+    unsigned char b = (unsigned char)( ( inWeight256 * c.b ) >> 8 );
+
 
     return r << 16 | g << 8 | b;
     }
@@ -453,24 +474,27 @@ rgbColor sampleFromWorldNoWeight( int inX, int inY, char *outTransient ) {
                 // in non-transparent part
             
                 if( blendWeight != 0 ) {
-                    returnColor.r = 
-                        ( 1 - blendWeight ) * a.mGraphics->mRed[ animIndex ] 
+                    // DOS-PORT: this blend runs only on a cache miss
+                    // (see rgbColorStruct's own comment) -- double math
+                    // here is fine, just round to int once at the end.
+                    returnColor.r = (int)(
+                        ( 1 - blendWeight ) * a.mGraphics->mRed[ animIndex ]
                         +
-                        blendWeight * a.mGraphics->mRed[ animBlendIndex ];
-                    returnColor.g = 
-                        ( 1 - blendWeight ) * a.mGraphics->mGreen[ animIndex ] 
+                        blendWeight * a.mGraphics->mRed[ animBlendIndex ] );
+                    returnColor.g = (int)(
+                        ( 1 - blendWeight ) * a.mGraphics->mGreen[ animIndex ]
                         +
-                        blendWeight * a.mGraphics->mGreen[ animBlendIndex ];
-                    returnColor.b = 
-                        ( 1 - blendWeight ) * a.mGraphics->mBlue[ animIndex ] 
+                        blendWeight * a.mGraphics->mGreen[ animBlendIndex ] );
+                    returnColor.b = (int)(
+                        ( 1 - blendWeight ) * a.mGraphics->mBlue[ animIndex ]
                         +
-                        blendWeight * a.mGraphics->mBlue[ animBlendIndex ];
+                        blendWeight * a.mGraphics->mBlue[ animBlendIndex ] );
                     }
                 else {
                     // no blend
-                    returnColor.r = a.mGraphics->mRed[ animIndex ];
-                    returnColor.g = a.mGraphics->mGreen[ animIndex ];
-                    returnColor.b = a.mGraphics->mBlue[ animIndex ];
+                    returnColor.r = (int)a.mGraphics->mRed[ animIndex ];
+                    returnColor.g = (int)a.mGraphics->mGreen[ animIndex ];
+                    returnColor.b = (int)a.mGraphics->mBlue[ animIndex ];
                     }
                 
                 *outTransient = true;
@@ -590,17 +614,19 @@ rgbColor sampleFromWorldNoWeight( int inX, int inY, char *outTransient ) {
     int imageIndex =  tileImageOffset + imageY * tileImageW + imageX;
     int blendImageIndex =  blendTileImageOffset + imageY * tileImageW + imageX;
     
-    returnColor.r = 
+    // DOS-PORT: cache-miss-only (see rgbColorStruct's own comment) --
+    // double blend math is fine here, just round to int once at the end.
+    returnColor.r = (int)(
         (1-blendWeight) * tileContainer->mRed[ imageIndex ] +
-        blendWeight * tileContainer->mRed[ blendImageIndex ];
-    
-    returnColor.g =  
+        blendWeight * tileContainer->mRed[ blendImageIndex ] );
+
+    returnColor.g = (int)(
         (1-blendWeight) * tileContainer->mGreen[ imageIndex ] +
-        blendWeight * tileContainer->mGreen[ blendImageIndex ];
-    
-    returnColor.b =  
+        blendWeight * tileContainer->mGreen[ blendImageIndex ] );
+
+    returnColor.b = (int)(
         (1-blendWeight) * tileContainer->mBlue[ imageIndex ] +
-        blendWeight * tileContainer->mBlue[ blendImageIndex ];
+        blendWeight * tileContainer->mBlue[ blendImageIndex ] );
     
     
     // only consider drawing chests in empty spots
@@ -665,9 +691,9 @@ rgbColor sampleFromWorldNoWeight( int inX, int inY, char *outTransient ) {
                     
                     gemColorUsed = true;
                     
-                    returnColor.r = gemColors[ gemNumber ][ 0 ];
-                    returnColor.g = gemColors[ gemNumber ][ 1 ];
-                    returnColor.b = gemColors[ gemNumber ][ 2 ];
+                    returnColor.r = (int)gemColors[ gemNumber ][ 0 ];
+                    returnColor.g = (int)gemColors[ gemNumber ][ 1 ];
+                    returnColor.b = (int)gemColors[ gemNumber ][ 2 ];
 
                     }
                 
@@ -676,9 +702,9 @@ rgbColor sampleFromWorldNoWeight( int inX, int inY, char *outTransient ) {
 
             if( !gemColorUsed ) {
                 // use underlying chest color
-                returnColor.r = chestContainer->mRed[ imageIndex ];
-                returnColor.g = chestContainer->mGreen[ imageIndex ];
-                returnColor.b = chestContainer->mBlue[ imageIndex ];
+                returnColor.r = (int)chestContainer->mRed[ imageIndex ];
+                returnColor.g = (int)chestContainer->mGreen[ imageIndex ];
+                returnColor.b = (int)chestContainer->mBlue[ imageIndex ];
                 }
             
             }
diff --git a/gameSource/World.h b/gameSource/World.h
index 452865a..efc8913 100644
--- a/gameSource/World.h
+++ b/gameSource/World.h
@@ -13,7 +13,10 @@ void initWorld();
 void destroyWorld();
         
 
-Uint32 sampleFromWorld( int inX, int inY, double inWeight = 1.0 );
+// DOS-PORT: inWeight256 is a fixed-point blend weight scaled by 256
+// (256 == a weight of 1.0) rather than a double -- see World.cpp's own
+// comment on sampleFromWorld() for why.
+Uint32 sampleFromWorld( int inX, int inY, int inWeight256 = 256 );
 
 
 void startPrizeAnimation( int inX, int inY );
diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index 61d6154..a32c478 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -1063,8 +1063,8 @@ char playGame() {
         for( int y=0; y<muralHeight; y++ ) {
             int worldX = x + s * stripWidth;
             
-            Uint32 sample = 
-                sampleFromWorld( worldX, y + yOffset, 1 );
+            Uint32 sample =
+                sampleFromWorld( worldX, y + yOffset, 256 );
             
             int muralY = s * (muralHeight + betweenStripSkip) + y;
             
@@ -1162,17 +1162,24 @@ char playGame() {
         // pass before the y loop -- same math, same results (maxWorldX/
         // minWorldX tracking moves here too, harmless since it only reads
         // x-dependent values that don't change across y).
+        // DOS-PORT: aWeight/bWeight caches store a fixed-point weight
+        // (scaled by 256, i.e. 256 == a weight of 1.0) rather than a
+        // double -- see World.cpp's sampleFromWorld() and
+        // rgbColorStruct's own comments for why: converting once here
+        // (100 times/frame) instead of leaving sampleFromWorld() to
+        // convert on every one of its own 2400 calls/frame is the whole
+        // point of this patch.
         static int *intWorldXCache = NULL;
-        static double *aWeightCache = NULL;
-        static double *bWeightCache = NULL;
+        static int *aWeight256Cache = NULL;
+        static int *bWeight256Cache = NULL;
         static int worldXCacheCapacity = 0;
         if( width > worldXCacheCapacity ) {
             delete [] intWorldXCache;
-            delete [] aWeightCache;
-            delete [] bWeightCache;
+            delete [] aWeight256Cache;
+            delete [] bWeight256Cache;
             intWorldXCache = new int[ width ];
-            aWeightCache = new double[ width ];
-            bWeightCache = new double[ width ];
+            aWeight256Cache = new int[ width ];
+            bWeight256Cache = new int[ width ];
             worldXCacheCapacity = width;
             }
 
@@ -1242,11 +1249,20 @@ char playGame() {
             int intWorldX = (int)floor( worldX );
 
             double bWeight = worldX - intWorldX;
-            double aWeight = 1 - bWeight;
+
+            // fixed-point, scaled by 256 -- bWeight256 truncates (matching
+            // the original double path's own truncating cast in
+            // sampleFromWorld), aWeight256 = 256 - bWeight256 (not a
+            // separate truncation of (1-bWeight)) so the two always sum
+            // to exactly 256, mirroring the original aWeight = 1-bWeight
+            // relationship exactly rather than letting two independent
+            // roundings drift apart.
+            int bWeight256 = (int)( bWeight * 256 );
+            int aWeight256 = 256 - bWeight256;
 
             intWorldXCache[x] = intWorldX;
-            aWeightCache[x] = aWeight;
-            bWeightCache[x] = bWeight;
+            aWeight256Cache[x] = aWeight256;
+            bWeight256Cache[x] = bWeight256;
             }
 
         for( int y=0; y<height; y++ ) {
@@ -1255,13 +1271,13 @@ char playGame() {
                 int worldY = (int)floor( y + dY );
 
                 int intWorldX = intWorldXCache[x];
-                double aWeight = aWeightCache[x];
-                double bWeight = bWeightCache[x];
+                int aWeight256 = aWeight256Cache[x];
+                int bWeight256 = bWeight256Cache[x];
 
                 Uint32 sampleA =
-                    sampleFromWorld( intWorldX, worldY, aWeight );
+                    sampleFromWorld( intWorldX, worldY, aWeight256 );
                 Uint32 sampleB =
-                    sampleFromWorld( intWorldX + 1, worldY, bWeight );
+                    sampleFromWorld( intWorldX + 1, worldY, bWeight256 );
 
 
 
